## Selected tool hooks
{% if "black" in optional_deps %}

### black — Code formatter

[black](https://black.readthedocs.io) reformats Python code to a consistent style.
It is opinionated and non-configurable by design (line length aside).

```bash
# Auto-format the entire project
black src/ tests/
```

**What it changes:** indentation, string quotes, trailing commas, blank lines between
functions/classes, long line wrapping.

> If `black` and `ruff` are both enabled, run `black` first — `ruff` understands black's
> formatting and will not conflict.
{% endif %}
{% if "ruff" in optional_deps %}

### ruff — Fast linter and formatter

[ruff](https://docs.astral.sh/ruff) is an extremely fast Python linter written in Rust.
It replaces `flake8`, `isort`, and several other tools. The hook runs with `--fix` so
auto-fixable violations are corrected in place.

```bash
# Lint and auto-fix
ruff check src/ tests/ --fix

# Format (ruff's own formatter)
ruff format src/ tests/
```

Common rule categories enabled by default: `E`/`W` (pycodestyle), `F` (pyflakes),
`UP` (pyupgrade), `I` (isort).  Configure in `pyproject.toml`:

```toml
[tool.ruff]
line-length = 100

[tool.ruff.lint]
select = ["E", "F", "W", "I", "UP"]
ignore = ["E501"]
```
{% endif %}
{% if "flake8" in optional_deps %}

### flake8 — Style linter

[flake8](https://flake8.pycqa.org) checks Python code for PEP 8 style violations,
undefined names, and unused imports.

```bash
flake8 src/ tests/
```

Configure in `pyproject.toml` or `.flake8`:

```toml
[tool.flake8]
max-line-length = 100
extend-ignore = ["E203"]
```
{% endif %}
{% if "isort" in optional_deps %}

### isort — Import sorter

[isort](https://pycqa.github.io/isort) automatically sorts and groups `import` statements
(stdlib → third-party → local) following PEP 8.

```bash
# Sort imports in place
isort src/ tests/
```

Configure in `pyproject.toml` to match your formatter:

```toml
[tool.isort]
profile = "black"   # compatible with black's style
```
{% endif %}
{% if "pyupgrade" in optional_deps %}

### pyupgrade — Syntax upgrader

[pyupgrade](https://github.com/asottile/pyupgrade) automatically rewrites Python code to
use modern syntax available in Python 3.13+. Examples:

- `"%s" % x` → `f"{x}"`
- `dict()` → `{}`
- old-style `super(ClassName, self)` → `super()`
- `Union[X, Y]` → `X | Y`

This hook makes no semantic changes — it only adopts syntax the current Python version
supports natively.
{% endif %}
{% if "mypy" in optional_deps %}

### mypy — Static type checker

[mypy](https://mypy.readthedocs.io) analyses type annotations and catches type errors
before runtime. The hook is configured with `--strict` and excludes `tests/`.

```bash
# Run mypy manually
mypy src/

# With full strict mode
mypy src/ --strict
```

Add inline overrides when a third-party library has no stubs:

```python
import some_untyped_lib  # type: ignore[import-untyped]
```

Add missing stubs as dev dependencies where available:

```bash
pip install types-requests types-python-dateutil
```
{% endif %}
{% if "bandit" in optional_deps %}

### bandit — Security linter

[bandit](https://bandit.readthedocs.io) scans Python code for common security issues
(SQL injection patterns, use of `eval`, hardcoded passwords, insecure hash algorithms, etc.).

The hook targets `src/` and runs quietly (`-q`):

```bash
# Run manually
bandit -r src/

# Verbose output with more detail
bandit -r src/ -v
```

To suppress a specific finding that is a false positive, add a comment:

```python
password = get_env("DB_PASSWORD")  # nosec B105
```

See [bandit issue codes](https://bandit.readthedocs.io/en/latest/plugins/index.html) for
the full list of checks.
{% endif %}
{% if "detect-secrets" in optional_deps %}

### detect-secrets — Secret scanner

[detect-secrets](https://github.com/Yelp/detect-secrets) scans every file for patterns
that look like secrets (API keys, tokens, passwords, connection strings).

**First-time setup** — create a baseline so existing non-secrets are acknowledged:

```bash
detect-secrets scan > .secrets.baseline
git add .secrets.baseline
```

The hook then only flags *new* secrets introduced after the baseline was created.

To update the baseline (e.g. after adding a legitimate-looking config value):

```bash
detect-secrets scan --update .secrets.baseline
```

To audit the current baseline interactively:

```bash
detect-secrets audit .secrets.baseline
```

> Add `.secrets.baseline` to version control — it's safe to commit and lets the team
> share the same allowlist.
{% endif %}

---

## Skipping hooks

To bypass all hooks for a single commit (use sparingly):

```bash
git commit --no-verify -m "WIP: work in progress"
```

To skip a specific hook only:

```bash
SKIP=mypy git commit -m "feat: add feature"
```

---

## Troubleshooting

| Symptom | Fix |
|---------|-----|
| Hook not found / wrong version | `pre-commit clean && pre-commit install` |
| Hooks run but don't fix files | Most hooks auto-fix; stage the changes (`git add .`) and commit again |
| `mypy` reports missing stubs | `pip install types-<package>` for the affected library |
{% if "detect-secrets" in optional_deps %}
| `detect-secrets` blocks a false positive | Run `detect-secrets scan --update .secrets.baseline` |
{% endif %}
| Want to ignore a specific file | Add `exclude: ^path/to/file` under the hook in `.pre-commit-config.yaml` |
